  "query_preview": "..A weak shortwave will push towards the east through \ntonight. This wave will help to trigger a few showers and \nthunderstorms, mainly towards the White Mountains and across far \neastern Arizona through this afternoon. Outside of these isolated \nstorms, we expect warm temperatures to continue with generally light \nwinds across the region. \n\nBy Friday, the trough exits to the east as a ridge of high pressure \ntries to build into the area. The ridge is weak and doesn't really \nget established as a trough starts to develop over the Pacific \nNorthwest and digs southward. A few lingering showers may develop on \nFriday across the higher terrain but expect most areas to remain dry \nwith warm temperatures continuing. \n\nAs we head into the weekend, we start to see a shift in the weather \nacross the Desert Southwest as the Pacific Northwest trough starts \nto dig to the south/southwest. As the trough digs south, it will \ninteract with what will become the remnants of TS Rachel. Current \nmodel runs continue to drag that moisture up into the Desert \nSouthwest over the weekend and into early next week. The trough \nlooks to become cut off by late in the weekend and models are slow \nto push the low off to the north and east. Additional tropical \nmoisture could be pulled northward into the region through at least \nmidweek, so it looks like we could have a very active and wet period \nahead of us. QPF ranges are fairly widespread at this time but the \npotential for 1-3\" with locally higher amounts remain possible. \nDepending on where the trough ends up and how the moisture is \nadvected into the region, will determine the areas with the biggest \nimpact from the system. Current projections have western areas \nseeing the greatest potential for heavy rain but any slight shifts \nin the track could push things to the east. As Rachel weakens and \nthe trough starts to dig south, we should start to see a better \npicture of just where the greatest impacts will be expected. On top \nof the potential for heavy rainfall, we will see increasing south to \nsouthwest winds with gusts 35-45 mph on Sunday. Keep any eye on the \nforecast as we get more refined over the next day or two.",
